<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Gediminids</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Gediminid dynasty</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Gediminids were the dynasty of grand dukes of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lithuania">Lithuania</a> descended from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gediminas">Gediminas</a>, who ruled from about 1316. Its members ruled Lithuania and, through the Jagiellonian branch descended from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/w-adys-aw-ii-jagie-o">Jogaila</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bohemia">Bohemia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hungary">Hungary</a>. Many princely families of Lithuania, Poland and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a>, among them the Czartoryski, Golitsyn and Trubetskoy, also traced their descent from Gediminas.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/gediminids.jpg" alt="Gediminids" width="320" height="320" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Лобачев Владимир. Base: File:Recueil d'armoiries polonaises - COA of Columns.png · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Recueil_d%27armoiries_polonaises_-_Columns_of_Gediminas.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Under Traidenis and later Vytenis the state regained strength, and around 1316 Vytenis's brother Gediminas, who gave his name to the <b>Gediminid dynasty</b>, succeeded to a grand duchy that was ready to expand into the lands of Rus' weakened by the Mongol invasions.</blockquote><a href="/empire/grand-duchy-of-lithuania">Grand Duchy of Lithuania · Foundation</a></li></ul></section>
